WEBVTT

00:00:00.261 --> 00:00:02.610
Here are two metal spheres, each with a

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:05.560
mass of seventy kilograms, with their centres one

00:00:05.661 --> 00:00:06.444
metre apart.

00:00:07.166 --> 00:00:09.049
Gravity pulls them together with a force of

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:11.715
about three point three times ten to the

00:00:11.796 --> 00:00:12.898
minus seven newtons.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.126
That's about the weight of a fine grain

00:00:15.147 --> 00:00:15.588
of sand.

00:00:16.412 --> 00:00:17.956
Put one of them on the ground, and

00:00:18.077 --> 00:00:19.881
Earth pulls it down with about six hundred

00:00:19.902 --> 00:00:20.824
and ninety newtons.

00:00:21.447 --> 00:00:24.013
So what makes Earth's pull about two billion

00:00:24.054 --> 00:00:26.839
times stronger, its mass or its distance?

00:00:27.564 --> 00:00:27.971
Have a think.

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:35.281
Newton's law of universal gravitation says every mass

00:00:35.382 --> 00:00:36.767
attracts every other mass.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.414
The force is big G times the two

00:00:39.455 --> 00:00:42.341
masses, divided by the distance between them squared.

00:00:43.123 --> 00:00:45.947
Big G is the gravitational constant, six point

00:00:45.988 --> 00:00:48.171
six seven times ten to the minus eleven,

00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:51.136
and it's tiny, which is why two everyday

00:00:51.197 --> 00:00:52.619
objects barely attract at all.

00:00:53.541 --> 00:00:55.805
The force pulls each mass straight towards the

00:00:55.866 --> 00:00:58.189
other, and by Newton's third law, the two

00:00:58.230 --> 00:00:59.973
forces are equal and opposite.

00:01:00.715 --> 00:01:02.620
So the sphere pulls the whole Earth up

00:01:02.721 --> 00:01:04.385
with six hundred and ninety newtons too.

00:01:05.477 --> 00:01:07.325
The key detail is that r is the

00:01:07.387 --> 00:01:08.733
distance between the centres.

00:01:09.297 --> 00:01:10.666
But for a sphere sitting on Earth,

00:01:11.291 --> 00:01:12.136
r isn't zero.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.414
It's Earth's radius, about six point four million

00:01:15.435 --> 00:01:15.776
metres.

00:01:16.705 --> 00:01:17.333
Why the centre?

00:01:18.123 --> 00:01:20.459
Earth's mass is spread through a huge ball.

00:01:21.103 --> 00:01:23.143
Rock on the near side pulls harder, rock

00:01:23.164 --> 00:01:25.004
on the far side pulls more weakly and

00:01:25.045 --> 00:01:25.504
at an angle,

00:01:26.086 --> 00:01:28.166
and for a spherical body, all those pulls

00:01:28.187 --> 00:01:30.187
add up to exactly what you'd get if

00:01:30.388 --> 00:01:31.928
all the mass sat at the centre.

00:01:32.712 --> 00:01:34.923
That only works outside the surface, which is

00:01:35.165 --> 00:01:35.969
everywhere we'll use it.

00:01:36.615 --> 00:01:38.804
Now the two factors, starting with mass.

00:01:39.387 --> 00:01:41.715
The force is proportional to each mass.

00:01:42.438 --> 00:01:44.707
Double Earth's mass and the pull doubles.

00:01:45.311 --> 00:01:46.916
Double the sphere's mass as well and it

00:01:46.977 --> 00:01:47.578
doubles again.

00:01:48.342 --> 00:01:50.493
So a bigger mass on either end means

00:01:50.534 --> 00:01:52.262
a proportionally stronger pull.

00:01:53.196 --> 00:01:55.407
Distance works the other way, and more strongly,

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:56.674
because it's squared.

00:01:57.497 --> 00:02:00.222
Picture gravity's influence spreading out from a mass

00:02:00.604 --> 00:02:02.067
over bigger and bigger spheres.

00:02:02.909 --> 00:02:05.173
Double the distance and the same influence covers

00:02:05.294 --> 00:02:07.758
four times the area, so the force falls

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:08.419
to a quarter.

00:02:09.224 --> 00:02:10.875
Triple it, and the force drops to a

00:02:10.977 --> 00:02:11.258
ninth.

00:02:11.945 --> 00:02:13.638
That's the inverse square law.

00:02:14.224 --> 00:02:15.914
It's a picture, not a proof, but it

00:02:16.036 --> 00:02:17.001
matches what we measure.

00:02:17.805 --> 00:02:18.715
How would you test that?

00:02:19.542 --> 00:02:21.949
Keep both masses fixed and measure the force

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:23.233
at several separations.

00:02:23.875 --> 00:02:26.046
A graph of force against distance is a

00:02:26.127 --> 00:02:28.297
steep curve that never quite reaches zero.

00:02:28.939 --> 00:02:31.381
But plot force against one over r squared,

00:02:31.782 --> 00:02:33.563
and the points fall on a straight line

00:02:33.603 --> 00:02:34.244
through the origin.

00:02:35.006 --> 00:02:37.191
That straight line is the evidence that force

00:02:37.272 --> 00:02:39.517
is proportional to one over r squared.

00:02:40.202 --> 00:02:41.329
Now back to our puzzle.

00:02:42.012 --> 00:02:44.796
Earth's centre is six point four million times

00:02:44.876 --> 00:02:46.739
further away than the other sphere's,

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.343
and squaring that weakens the pull by a

00:02:49.403 --> 00:02:50.104
factor of about

00:02:50.605 --> 00:02:52.508
four times ten to the thirteen.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:55.675
But Earth's mass is nearly ten to the

00:02:55.736 --> 00:02:58.162
twenty-three times bigger than the sphere's.

00:02:58.885 --> 00:03:00.822
Mass wins by about two billion.

00:03:01.507 --> 00:03:04.453
So the answer is Earth's enormous mass,

00:03:05.016 --> 00:03:06.639
even though its distance works against it.

00:03:07.475 --> 00:03:09.662
The force changes with whatever mass you put

00:03:09.703 --> 00:03:12.130
there, so to describe gravity at a place,

00:03:12.452 --> 00:03:14.116
we divide by the small mass.

00:03:14.659 --> 00:03:17.211
That gives the gravitational field strength, little g,

00:03:17.715 --> 00:03:19.262
the force on each kilogram.

00:03:19.944 --> 00:03:22.469
Little g equals big G times the planet's

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:25.275
mass over r squared, and the small mass

00:03:25.316 --> 00:03:25.937
has cancelled.

00:03:26.719 --> 00:03:29.344
Its units are newtons per kilogram, the same

00:03:29.385 --> 00:03:31.749
as metres per second squared, so it's also

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:33.432
the free-fall acceleration there.

00:03:34.036 --> 00:03:36.352
It's a vector, pointing towards the planet's centre.

00:03:37.262 --> 00:03:39.672
Let's predict g at Earth's surface, using the

00:03:39.712 --> 00:03:41.419
values on the HSC data sheet.

00:03:42.102 --> 00:03:44.203
Big G is six point six seven times

00:03:44.263 --> 00:03:45.244
ten to the minus eleven,

00:03:45.784 --> 00:03:47.885
Earth's mass is six point zero times ten

00:03:47.905 --> 00:03:50.686
to the twenty-four kilograms, and r is Earth's

00:03:50.766 --> 00:03:53.347
radius, six point three seven one times ten

00:03:53.367 --> 00:03:54.208
to the six metres.

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:56.960
Then g comes out at about nine point

00:03:57.062 --> 00:03:58.529
eight six newtons per kilogram.

00:03:58.972 --> 00:04:00.417
That's close to the nine point eight we

00:04:00.478 --> 00:04:01.079
usually use.

00:04:01.662 --> 00:04:04.027
Real values vary a little, because Earth isn't

00:04:04.048 --> 00:04:05.872
a perfect sphere and it spins.

00:04:06.495 --> 00:04:08.940
Now go four hundred kilometres up, where the

00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:09.842
space station flies.

00:04:10.505 --> 00:04:12.735
The classic mistake is to put four hundred

00:04:12.776 --> 00:04:14.382
kilometres in for r.

00:04:15.066 --> 00:04:17.350
That gives a field thousands of times stronger

00:04:17.432 --> 00:04:19.054
than at the surface, which can't be right.

00:04:20.198 --> 00:04:22.563
r is measured from the centre, so add

00:04:22.604 --> 00:04:25.710
the radius, and r is six point seven

00:04:25.771 --> 00:04:28.056
seven one times ten to the six metres.

00:04:28.798 --> 00:04:31.085
Then g is about eight point seven three

00:04:31.266 --> 00:04:34.214
newtons per kilogram, still about eighty-nine per cent

00:04:34.235 --> 00:04:35.137
of its surface value.

00:04:36.248 --> 00:04:37.039
Here's the whole picture.

00:04:37.702 --> 00:04:39.949
The field arrows all point to Earth's centre,

00:04:40.371 --> 00:04:42.799
crowded close to the surface and spreading apart

00:04:42.879 --> 00:04:43.401
further out.

00:04:44.203 --> 00:04:46.590
At two Earth radii from the centre, which

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:47.813
is one radius up,

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:50.480
g is a quarter of its surface value,

00:04:50.881 --> 00:04:51.944
about two point five.

00:04:52.767 --> 00:04:54.675
At three radii it's a ninth.

00:04:55.338 --> 00:04:57.241
It keeps getting weaker, but it never switches

00:04:57.342 --> 00:04:59.286
off at any distance, it just tends towards

00:04:59.327 --> 00:04:59.607
zero.

00:05:00.742 --> 00:05:02.829
To keep it straight: measure r from the

00:05:02.909 --> 00:05:03.150
middle.

00:05:03.812 --> 00:05:05.880
Twice as far, a quarter of the pull.

00:05:06.764 --> 00:05:08.519
And keep the two equations apart.

00:05:09.104 --> 00:05:10.772
The force uses both masses.

00:05:11.394 --> 00:05:13.961
Field strength uses only the planet's mass, because

00:05:14.061 --> 00:05:15.805
it's the force on each kilogram.

00:05:18.513 --> 00:05:18.940
Your turn.

00:05:19.829 --> 00:05:21.852
Mars has a mass of six point four

00:05:21.933 --> 00:05:24.718
two times ten to the twenty-three kilograms and

00:05:24.739 --> 00:05:26.962
a radius of three point four zero times

00:05:27.043 --> 00:05:28.325
ten to the six metres.

00:05:29.047 --> 00:05:31.669
Calculate the gravitational field strength at its surface,

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:34.151
and express it as a percentage of Earth's

00:05:34.192 --> 00:05:35.613
value of nine point eight.

00:05:36.577 --> 00:05:37.843
Pause here to answer.

00:05:48.654 --> 00:05:51.157
Use g equals big G M over r

00:05:51.218 --> 00:05:53.139
squared, with the radius squared.

00:05:53.801 --> 00:05:56.223
That gives about three point seven zero newtons

00:05:56.264 --> 00:05:58.706
per kilogram, around thirty-eight per cent of Earth's.

00:05:59.488 --> 00:06:01.871
The slip to avoid is forgetting to square

00:06:01.912 --> 00:06:02.432
the radius.

00:06:04.226 --> 00:06:04.654
Last one.

00:06:05.242 --> 00:06:07.928
At what altitude above Earth's surface is the

00:06:07.968 --> 00:06:11.156
gravitational field strength one quarter of its value

00:06:11.377 --> 00:06:11.999
at the surface?

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:15.047
Give your answer in metres, using Earth's radius

00:06:15.167 --> 00:06:17.554
of six point three seven one times ten

00:06:17.594 --> 00:06:18.577
to the six metres.

00:06:19.301 --> 00:06:20.174
Pause here to answer.

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:32.926
A quarter of the field needs double the

00:06:32.946 --> 00:06:35.773
distance from the centre, so r is two

00:06:35.974 --> 00:06:36.696
Earth radii.

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:40.223
The altitude is r minus the radius, which

00:06:40.283 --> 00:06:42.928
is one Earth radius, six point three seven

00:06:43.049 --> 00:06:44.972
one times ten to the six metres.

00:06:45.774 --> 00:06:48.377
The classic slip is answering two Earth radii,

00:06:48.497 --> 00:06:50.559
which is the distance from the centre, not

00:06:50.599 --> 00:06:51.260
the altitude.

00:06:53.301 --> 00:06:55.843
So four hundred kilometres up, gravity is still

00:06:55.923 --> 00:06:57.866
almost ninety per cent as strong as on

00:06:57.906 --> 00:06:58.366
the ground.

00:06:59.148 --> 00:07:01.096
Then why doesn't a satellite up there fall

00:07:01.157 --> 00:07:01.739
straight down?

00:07:02.524 --> 00:07:04.741
It's moving sideways, very fast.

00:07:05.446 --> 00:07:07.393
Next time, we'll see how gravity keeps it

00:07:07.434 --> 00:07:08.699
going round in a circle.

00:07:11.395 --> 00:07:12.217
Here are the key notes.

00:07:12.741 --> 00:07:14.288
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:14.329 --> 00:07:14.750
to copy them.
